One important thing to note is that the Boy Scouts of America recently released their

51
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:06,080
initial implementation resources addressing the change to membership standards voted on

52
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,900
at this year's meeting in May.

53
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:13,480
Part of the resources that they've provided to date is a frequently asked questions for

54
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:18,640
unit leaders document, and I'll make sure to link to it in the post that contains this

55
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:22,520
podcast, and I mentioned it on the blog within the past week or so.

56
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:27,680
You want to make sure to look at that because it provides initial guidance on implementation

57
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:33,880
of this membership change and just to give you a condensed overview. not much changes

58
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:38,640
With anything at all in the day-to-day workings of a troop program.

59
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:43,800
As always, we always apply our training and available resources to presenting the program

60
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:48,560
in the way that best benefits and serves the interests of our Scouts.

61
00:04:48,560 --> 00:04:53,960
When this change goes into effect at the first of next year, our task as leaders is to

62
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:59,240
address any practical concerns that arise once it does, and the way that we do that

63
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:01,600
is the way that we do everything else:

64
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:05,800
By asking the question: what's in the best interest of the youth that we serve? and we

65
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:12,040
apply the good judgment that we've developed over our experience and through our training.

66
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:16,720
Now, as the debate was brewing this past spring, questions of accommodations and

