additional training material to be released this fall.

80
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:17,400
I'll keep my eyes open for it and share any further information with you, both on

81
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,120
the blog and here in the podcast.

82
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:22,920
In this podcast and Scout Master's ship in seven minutes or less,

83
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:27,640
we're going to talk about sharing information with our youth leaders.

84
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,039
We do this in training.

85
00:06:29,039 --> 00:06:30,440
We do it in mentoring.

86
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:33,240
We're going to talk a little bit about how we go about doing that effectively,

87
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:34,919
and that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

88
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:36,280
So let's get started, shall we?

89
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:06,440
Scout Master's ship in seven minutes or less.

90
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,080
So we're working with our youth leaders. when we're mentoring them or

91
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:18,520
training them or advising them,

92
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:21,800
We're doing a couple of different things.

93
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:27,240
First of all, we're sharing information with them, and information is great.

94
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:31,080
It educates and directs and helps make things clearer,

95
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:35,400
And we know that having that information is good,

96
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:39,560
but it's only experience that develops skill.

97
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:42,760
Sharing information is good, but I'll tell you something,

98
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:45,240
especially when it relates to our youth leadership,

99
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:48,840
too much information can be paralyzing.

100
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:51,960
We live in the information age.

101
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:56,600
Our scouts get plenty of information and much more than any of us can

102
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,440
effectively do anything with.

103
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:04,040
What we really need is being able to take what information we receive

104
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:07,240
and apply it and gain experience from that.

105
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:09,800
Scouting is an educational movement,

106
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:13,240
but it's not educational in the traditional sense.

107
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:17,640
Scouting is a journey through experiences that develop skill.

108
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:23,960
It's not just this body of information that we impart to our scouts.

109
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:26,360
Sometimes we get a little mixed up.

110
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:30,680
We think our scouts need a lot of information to develop skills

111
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:31,720
and they don't.

112
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:34,200
I think we get a little mixed up when we're talking about

113
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:35,640
training adult leaders with this.

114
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:37,560
We think they need lots of information.

115
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:38,919
They don't.

116
00:08:38,919 --> 00:08:41,800
What they really need is experience,

117
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,160
and this is true of almost everything we do in scouting.

118
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:48,280
So if you have ever taught someone how to ride a bike,

119
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:49,480
think about that for a moment.

120
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:50,920
What would you do?

121
00:08:50,920 --> 00:08:53,320
We get a bike out, we put them in the seat,

122
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:54,600
we give them a couple of pointers

123
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:56,040
and we tell them to try it out.

124
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:58,280
The first couple of tries aren't going to work very well,

125
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:01,240
probably, but as they start to figure things out,

126
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:03,240
we coach them and we encourage them

127
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:07,640
and we work with them until, through experience,

128
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:12,920
they've developed enough skill to go ahead and ride a bike. right

129
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:17,000
When we're instructing scouts or working with scouts on a skill

130
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,680
like building a campfire or safely handling a pocket knife

131
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:21,959
or flipping a flapjack.

132
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:25,719
we're giving them the information that they need at the time

133
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:30,200
and they are applying it in a very hands-on kind of an atmosphere,

134
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:34,360
and there's not necessarily a lot of talking going on,

135
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:35,480
but there's a lot of action,

136
00:09:35,480 --> 00:09:38,040
there's a lot of experience being gained.

137
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:43,400
So how do we apply this to training or mentoring

138
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:46,680
or advising our youth leaders?

139
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:49,480
Unfortunately, a lot of our training,

140
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:50,920
both for youth and adults,

141
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:53,960
is aimed at sharing information.

142
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:57,480
More often than not, what we do is we plop people down

143
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:59,960
in front of a PowerPoint presentation or a video

144
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:03,080
and we give them lots of handouts

145
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:06,600
and then we say, okay, now you have the information,

146
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:07,640
now you've been trained.

147
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:11,000
But that is just the beginning, isn't it?

148
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:15,560
Once somebody has the information, they have to act on it.

149
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:17,960
When they act on it, they gain the experience.

150
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:20,600
When they gain the experience, then they become skilled,

151
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:25,800
and that very simple concept is what we are all about, isn't it?

152
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:28,280
Measuring out the information we share

153
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:31,320
with our youth leaders is important.

154
00:10:31,320 --> 00:10:33,960
It's not because we want to keep secrets

155
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:37,880
or we don't want to reveal to them what's going to happen next,

156
00:10:37,880 --> 00:10:43,000
but because we want them to get the amount of information

157
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,080
they need to act. and then what?

158
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:49,560
Gain an experience and develop skill.

159
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:52,440
So I have right now a new senior patrol leader

160
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:55,240
that was elected during our week at summer camp

161
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:58,680
and he had his first patrol leaders council meeting last week.

162
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:03,000
I could sit him down and I could sit the new patrol leaders council down

163
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,360
and I could start sharing information with them,

164
00:11:06,360 --> 00:11:09,880
to the point that they would become totally overloaded

165
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:12,040
and they're really not sure what to do next.

166
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:16,600
Now, because I am a talker and I love to talk,

167
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:19,480
I could have started out with their job descriptions

168
00:11:19,560 --> 00:11:23,480
and then probably followed 17 different rabbit trails

169
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:26,840
on each bullet point of those job descriptions.

170
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:29,560
Pretty soon, you know, about 10 minutes into

171
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:34,200
my sharing information, their eyes would kind of glaze over,

172
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:35,720
their heads would begin to nod,

173
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:37,480
their feet would begin to shuffle,

174
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:42,200
because those are all indications that I have overloaded them.

175
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:44,360
I have told them more than they need to know.

176
00:11:44,360 --> 00:11:47,640
What they're anxious for is the information

177
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:50,199
that they need to know. right then.

178
00:11:50,199 --> 00:11:53,000
What I did was I sat down with the senior patrol leader

179
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,360
for about five minutes

180
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:57,480
and I asked him some questions about the content

181
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:59,640
of his patrol leaders council meeting.

182
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:00,600
What are you going to do?

183
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:03,080
Do you have it written down?

184
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:04,920
If you write down what you're going to do at a meeting,

185
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,040
do you know what that's called?

186
00:12:06,040 --> 00:12:07,160
That's called an agenda.

187
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:09,720
An agenda is a pretty important thing to have.

188
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:11,400
If you share that agenda with people

189
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:12,600
before you get to the meeting,

190
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:14,520
they're going to be more prepared for it.

191
00:12:14,520 --> 00:12:16,280
Now is that something that you did,

192
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:19,480
And we went along like that for about five minutes.

193
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:21,640
We talked about the very practical things

194
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:23,000
that he needed to do,

195
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,680
and then I stopped talking.

196
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:26,120
I left the room.

197
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:28,040
I let the patrol leaders council go ahead

198
00:12:28,040 --> 00:12:30,040
and sit there and get to work.

199
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,199
I didn't sit in on their meeting.

200
00:12:32,199 --> 00:12:33,720
I didn't guide their meeting.

201
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:35,800
I didn't do anything else other than

202
00:12:36,439 --> 00:12:39,240
give them the pertinent information

203
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:41,480
they needed for the next half hour or so.

204
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:43,640
Now my next move was to go out

205
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:45,959
and to work with my junior assistant,

206
00:12:45,960 --> 00:12:47,000
Scott Masters.

207
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,160
They're 16 or 17 years old.

208
00:12:49,160 --> 00:12:51,160
They're very experienced guys.

209
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:53,480
They understand what all the youth leadership

210
00:12:53,480 --> 00:12:55,560
positions are and how to do them well,

211
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:57,960
And I gave them enough information

212
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:00,360
so that they could get started basically

213
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:03,480
doing my job right.

214
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:05,080
I'm going to be there

215
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:07,320
and I'm going to be working along with everybody,

216
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:10,600
but I want these junior assistant, Scott Masters

217
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:13,000
to work with the senior patrol leader

218
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:14,360
and the patrol leaders council

219
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:17,000
in the same way that I usually would.

220
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,560
But I have these great, experienced guys,

221
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:22,440
so it's time for them to go to the next level

222
00:13:22,440 --> 00:13:26,840
and to learn about the job that Scott Masters do.

223
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:27,960
Because what are they?

224
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:29,880
They're junior assistant Scott Masters.

225
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:31,960
So we talked about different ways

226
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:34,440
that they could support the patrol leaders council

227
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:36,120
and the senior patrol leader.

228
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:39,560
We talked about what I'm talking about right now,

229
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:41,480
which is, hey, if you're going to talk

230
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:43,480
to the senior patrol leader, remember

231
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:47,880
he doesn't need to know everything that you know right now.

232
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:49,880
He needs to know what he needs to know

233
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:54,200
for the next few things that he needs to accomplish

234
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:57,000
And what we're doing right now in our troop.

235
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,240
we've got a couple of different levels of this going.

236
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:04,040
Things go better if I give my youth leadership

237
00:14:04,040 --> 00:14:05,720
the most pertinent information

238
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:09,240
that they read right now to get their job done

239
00:14:09,240 --> 00:14:12,360
and let them work on that piece of information,

240
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:15,080
let them gain some experience from applying it.

241
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:18,440
then we'll reflect on that experience

242
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:21,480
and the developing skills that they have

243
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:23,960
and then they'll get the next bit of information:

244
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:25,560
Somebody learning how to ride a bike,

245
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:28,040
scouts learning how to handle a pocket knife

246
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:29,160
or build a fire.

247
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:33,800
They need brief, concise, informational statements

248
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:38,040
and then lots of practice and a little coaching

249
00:14:38,040 --> 00:14:39,640
as they practice along.

250
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:43,400
Then the final step is to get them to reflect

251
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:47,000
on the way that they applied the information that was shared.

252
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:48,680
How did it work?

253
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:49,400
How did it go?

254
00:14:49,400 --> 00:14:51,240
What are you going to do differently next time?

255
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:53,000
What do you think you did well?

256
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:55,480
What do you think you can improve on?

257
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:57,720
And we do this not an hour long sessions,

258
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,120
but kind of in real time.

259
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:01,480
We take a couple of minutes here,

260
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:02,840
a couple of minutes there.

261
00:15:02,840 --> 00:15:05,880
We have a patrol leaders council meeting once a month.

262
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:07,880
The patrol leaders council gets together

263
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:10,520
before the troop meeting to check on their preparations

264
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:13,480
and after the troop meeting to reflect on the meeting

265
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:15,880
and check on their preparations for next time.

266
00:15:15,880 --> 00:15:21,320
And in those times my most important tool is my ears.

267
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:23,480
I'm listening to what they say,

268
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:26,760
I'm listening to their discussions with one another,

269
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:29,960
I'm observing what they're doing during meetings,

270
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:33,000
during campouts, looking at the interactions

271
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:33,800
that are happening,

272
00:15:34,360 --> 00:15:36,360
And then I'm giving them the information

273
00:15:36,360 --> 00:15:40,680
I think they need to move on to the next step of skill development.

274
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:42,680
So let's remember, you know,

275
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:45,400
as denizens of the information age.

276
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:47,800
there's a lot of stuff out there.

277
00:15:47,800 --> 00:15:49,880
We can take all the information that we know.

278
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:51,800
we can dump it on somebody and say,

279
00:15:51,800 --> 00:15:54,680
okay, now you got it, see you later.

280
00:15:54,680 --> 00:15:58,920
But the best approach is that riding a bike approach right,

281
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:00,840
Giving them what they need right. then

282
00:16:01,480 --> 00:16:05,080
letting them digest that, internalize it,

283
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:08,200
take it, make it into experiences,

284
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:12,120
that sharpen skills, reflect on the experience

285
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:14,200
and help them see what they're getting right

286
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:16,520
and what they maybe need to improve on.

287
00:16:16,520 --> 00:16:19,000
And then we move on to the next challenge.

